| --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x+1) \\\\  & =1(-1+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2},1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & ={y}_{1}({x}_{1}+1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={y}_{2}({x}_{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\ =(-1+1,\\,1+\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot 1) \\\\ =(0,\\frac{13}{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & ={y}_{3}({x}_{3}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{8}\\cdot (0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{8}$ |  |\n\n- Finally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(0+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{8}+\\frac{13}{8})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{31}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{8}$ | $\\frac{31}{48}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{31}{48} \\\\  & =\\frac{79}{48}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{8}$ | $\\frac{31}{48}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{79}{48}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0)≈\\frac{79}{48}.$" 13194139535141 true] [17592186333392 1017 #edb/ref 17592186333393 13194139535141 true] 